FOLLOW-UP (2026-08-16): bitmap-subtitle edge smoothing added, matching a
user report against the same real-world .mp4 file this patch was
originally written for -- its dvd_subtitle-codec track looked "jaggy" for
the same reason DVD-disc subtitles do (see Vivace's own
PlayerController::softenSubtitleEdges(), added the same day for the
DVD-disc case, whose comment explains the underlying cause and the
triangular-kernel-not-box-blur choice in full). Since this pipeline is
entirely inside qtmultimedia with no way to call back into Vivace,
qffmpegstreamdecoder.cpp's decodeSubtitle() gained its OWN copy of the
same blur algorithm (vivaceSoftenBitmapSubtitle(), a static function
right above StreamDecoder::StreamDecoder()), applied to the decoded
QImage immediately after it's built from the palette bitmap, before it's
threaded any further through the pipeline described above -- so every
downstream consumer (Frame, SubtitleRenderer, QVideoSink, QSGVideoNode)
is unaffected and needs no changes. Controlled by the
VIVACE_SUBTITLE_BITMAP_SMOOTHING environment variable (0 = off, 1-3 =
increasing radius, read fresh via qEnvironmentVariableIntValue() on every
call rather than cached, so it can change without restarting) rather
than a compile-time constant, specifically so Vivace's existing
Preferences > Subtitles > DVD subtitles > Smoothing control (a QML/C++
Settings value with no way to reach into this patched Qt code directly)
can still govern it: PlayerController::setDvdSubtitleSmoothing() (Vivace
side, src/playercontroller.cpp) now also calls qputenv() with the same
value whenever it changes, so ONE Preferences control transparently
governs both the DVD-disc pipeline (Vivace's own code) and this
embedded-file pipeline (this patch). NOT YET independently re-verified
against a real rebuild in this environment -- same limitation as every
other change to this patch; needs the user to rebuild qtmultimedia and
Vivace and confirm the bitmap subtitle in the reported .mp4 file now
looks smoother with Smoothing > 0 and unchanged at Smoothing = 0.


INVESTIGATED 2026-08-17, NOT FIXED (real Blu-ray disc testing, PGS
subtitle tracks): selecting a Blu-ray subtitle track (hdmv_pgs_subtitle
codec) via Subtitles > Track correctly updates the active-track
selection/checkmark (confirmed: Vivace's own selection UI and
activeSubtitleTrack mechanism work correctly for Blu-ray, same as any
ordinary file), but never produces a visible subtitle at all, even at
real, confirmed subtitle-packet timestamps (verified directly via
ffprobe against the real disc's raw .m2ts -- genuine PGS packets with
non-trivial rect data exist at ~9s/~32s/~40s and beyond).

Root cause identified: this function's `if (end <= start) { ...; return;
}` check rejects every real PGS subtitle event unconditionally, because
FFmpeg's own hdmv_pgs_subtitle decoder leaves AVSubtitle::
end_display_time at 0 (PGS doesn't encode an explicit per-event
duration; it relies on a LATER, separate empty "clear" segment instead)
-- so subtitle.pts's start/end always collapse to the same value and the
bitmap-decode loop just below this check (added by the base version of
this patch) is never reached for Blu-ray content at all.

A fix was attempted: give a zero/negative-duration event with real rect
content (num_rects > 0) a several-second fallback duration instead of
discarding it, so it can reach the existing bitmap-decode loop. This
DID let the code reach that loop for the first time ever with real PGS
data -- but it also produced a real, reproducible crash on the exact
same real disc used to test it: a Debug CRT heap-validation failure
(_CrtIsValidHeapPointer, debug_heap.cpp:904), confirmed via cdb attached
to the running process. Investigation notes, so a future attempt
doesn't have to re-derive them:
  - The crash is NOT the `avsubtitle_free()` call an earlier version of
    this attempt also added in the num_rects==0 branch -- removing that
    call alone did not stop the crash on a retest against the same disc.
  - The crash correlates with a `QObject: Cannot create children for a
    parent that is in a different thread` warning naming Vivace's own
    PlayerController as the parent, appearing (per vivace.log) only
    once this fix lets a real PGS Frame flow through -- never in any
    earlier, successful Blu-ray session (checked directly: earlier
    preserved vivace.log captures from before this fix have zero
    occurrences of this warning or of any heap-corruption message).
  - The bitmap-decode loop itself (qsgvideonode_p.cpp's
    updateSubtitleImage(), reviewed directly) doesn't obviously explain
    the warning -- QSGImageNode/QSGTransformNode/QSGTexture are QSGNode-
    derived, not QObject-derived, so they shouldn't be able to trigger a
    QObject cross-thread-parenting warning by themselves. The actual
    trigger (something that constructs a real QObject parented to
    PlayerController from Qt Multimedia's own worker thread, apparently
    only once a subtitle-track StreamDecoder/Renderer actually processes
    a real Frame rather than always hitting an early return) was not
    pinned down further -- live debugging in this environment proved
    unreliable past this point (a Retry-button click meant to let cdb
    catch a real breakpoint blocked indefinitely via SendMessage rather
    than resolving).
  - FFmpeg's real pgssubdec.c source (fetched directly, n7.1 tag) was
    checked and RULED OUT two specific hypotheses: the palette buffer is
    always allocated at the full AVPALETTE_SIZE (256 entries) regardless
    of the real color count, same as DVD's decoder, so an out-of-bounds
    palette read isn't the cause; linesize[0] is set equal to the
    rect's own width with no padding, so an out-of-bounds bitmap-row
    read isn't the cause either.

Given the crash is worse than the original "silently doesn't render"
symptom, the fix attempt was REVERTED -- this function is back to its
original, base-patch behavior (unconditionally rejecting any
zero/negative-duration subtitle event). Blu-ray subtitle track selection
itself works correctly and is safe to use (confirmed no crash with a
real subtitle track selected and real content played through it); the
actual bitmap/text never appears on screen for Blu-ray (PGS) content
specifically. DVD/embedded-file bitmap subtitles are unaffected --
they don't hit this same codec-specific quirk and were not touched by
either the attempted fix or this revert.

CORRECTION 2026-08-17 (real evidence, not memory): the "Root cause
identified" paragraph above is WRONG about *why* `end <= start` fires.
Re-fetched the real n7.1 `libavcodec/pgssubdec.c` directly and read
`display_end_segment()`: it does NOT leave `end_display_time` at 0 --
it explicitly sets `sub->end_display_time = UINT32_MAX`, with its own
comment: "There is no explicit end time for PGS subtitles. The end
time is defined by the start of the next sub which may contain no
objects (i.e. clears the previous sub)." Traced this into
`decodeSubtitle()`'s arithmetic (`TrackPosition(*pts +
qint64(subtitle.end_display_time) * 1000)`, and `TrackPosition`'s own
`BaseTime` wrapper, `qtaggedtime_p.h`) and confirmed no overflow/
narrowing happens anywhere in that chain -- a `UINT32_MAX`
end_display_time genuinely produces an `end` about 49.7 days after
`start`, which should make `end <= start` FALSE, not true. Also
checked FFmpeg's own generic `avcodec_decode_subtitle2()` wrapper
(`libavcodec/decode.c`): it only overrides `end_display_time` from the
packet's own duration `if (!sub->end_display_time)` -- a no-op here,
since `UINT32_MAX` is non-zero/truthy.
So the ACTUAL trigger must be the *other* branch of `decodeSubtitle()`'s
if/else -- `subtitle.pts == AV_NOPTS_VALUE`, which uses
`packet.avPacket()->duration` instead (very plausibly 0 for a real BD
PGS elementary stream muxed over MPEG-TS/PES, which has no equivalent
per-packet "declared duration" field the way some container formats
do). A temporary diagnostic (two `qWarning()` calls, right where
`start`/`end` are computed) was added to nail this down with real
evidence before attempting another fix -- CONFIRMED below.

CONFIRMED FIXED 2026-08-17 (real diagnostic evidence from the user's own
rebuild against the same real disc, subtitle track selected): the
hypothesis above was exactly right. Every real vivace.log line showed
`subtitle.pts=-9223372036854775808` (`AV_NOPTS_VALUE`) with the
underlying packet's own `pts` perfectly valid, and
`packet.duration=0` -- confirming `decodeSubtitle()` was permanently
stuck in the `AV_NOPTS_VALUE` branch, where a Blu-ray PGS elementary
stream's always-zero packet duration collapses `end` to exactly
`start` every single time, regardless of the `end_display_time`
convention corrected above (that branch of the if/else is simply never
reached).

Traced *why* `subtitle.pts` never gets populated from the packet's own
valid `pts`: FFmpeg's generic `avcodec_decode_subtitle2()` wrapper
(`libavcodec/decode.c`, fetched directly) only rescales
`avpkt->pts` into `sub->pts` `if (avctx->pkt_timebase.num &&
avpkt->pts != AV_NOPTS_VALUE)` -- i.e. it requires the codec context's
own `pkt_timebase` to be set first. Read
`qffmpegcodeccontext.cpp`'s `CodecContext::create()` (the only place
Qt Multimedia builds an `AVCodecContext`) end to end and confirmed
`pkt_timebase` is never set anywhere for ANY stream type --
`avcodec_parameters_to_context()` doesn't set it either (checked
directly: it only copies codec parameters -- dimensions, sample rate,
extradata, etc. -- never timebase-related fields). So
`avctx->pkt_timebase.num` was always 0, the rescale was always
skipped, and `subtitle.pts` stayed `AV_NOPTS_VALUE` forever, for every
subtitle stream this backend has ever decoded (not Blu-ray-specific in
principle, just never exposed before because it happens to line up
with `packet.avPacket()->duration` also being 0 specifically for PGS
over MPEG-TS/PES -- other embedded-subtitle formats/containers used
elsewhere in Vivace evidently carry a usable packet duration and so
never hit this gap).

Fix (`qffmpegcodeccontext.cpp`, `CodecContext::create()`): right after
`avcodec_parameters_to_context()` succeeds, for a subtitle-type codec
context only (`context->codec_type == AVMEDIA_TYPE_SUBTITLE` --
scoped deliberately narrow, since audio/video already get correct
timing through other means in this codebase and don't need this),
set `context->pkt_timebase = stream->time_base;`. This lets
`avcodec_decode_subtitle2()` correctly rescale the packet's own valid
`pts` into `subtitle.pts`, which sends `decodeSubtitle()` down the
`else` branch instead -- using PGS's own real `UINT32_MAX`
`end_display_time` convention (see the CORRECTION above), so `end`
becomes about 49.7 days after `start` and the `end <= start` rejection
no longer fires for real content. This also correctly handles PGS's
later, empty "clear" segments (`num_rects == 0`): they reach the exact
same `else` branch and the same huge `end_display_time`, so they pass
this check too and fall through to `onFrameFound()` (called
unconditionally regardless of `num_rects`) with empty text and a null
image at their own correct, disc-authored time -- exactly what should
happen to clear the previous subtitle when the disc says to, with no
guessed fallback duration needed (unlike the earlier, reverted,
crash-causing attempt above, which tried to paper over the symptom
with a guessed several-second duration instead of fixing the actual
cause).

Verified this fix cannot introduce a new stall/scheduling bug from
such a huge duration: read `qffmpegrenderer.cpp`'s `Renderer::render()`/
`nextTimePoint()` and `qffmpegrenderer_p.h`'s `RenderingResult`
directly -- subtitle-frame scheduling is governed by the NEXT queued
frame's own `absolutePts()`, not by waiting out the CURRENT frame's
`duration`, and `SubtitleRenderer::renderInternal()` always returns a
`done=true` result, so each subtitle `Frame` (real or "clear") is
displayed then immediately dequeued regardless of how large its own
`duration` is. The only other place `end`/`absoluteEnd()` is consulted
is a staleness check in `Renderer::render()`
(`frame.absoluteEnd() < seekPosition()`), which simply becomes inert
(never true) for such a huge value -- harmless, not a correctness
issue, since staleness detection is not meaningful for a "this event
runs until further notice" PGS convention anyway.

The temporary `SUBTITLE DIAG`/`SUBTITLE DIAG2` `qWarning()` calls have
been removed from `qffmpegstreamdecoder.cpp` now that the cause is
confirmed rather than hypothesized.

VERIFIED WORKING END TO END 2026-08-17 (real interactive playback against
the real disc, subtitle track selected, rebuilt Vivace against the
rebuilt/reinstalled qtmultimedia): a real PGS bitmap subtitle rendered
correctly on screen for the first time ever -- a disc-authored English
translation of an on-screen Japanese credit line ("Producer / Toru
Hara"), correctly positioned near the bottom of the frame, tracking
forward as playback continued.

SECOND BUG found immediately by this same real-disc test, fixed the same
day (a genuinely different problem from the pkt_timebase one above, only
exposed once real PGS subtitle data started flowing through this
function for the first time): taking `end_display_time`'s UINT32_MAX
literally produced a duration of ~49.71 days, which caused a real,
complete PLAYBACK STALL a couple of real subtitle events into the film
-- video and audio both froze entirely, CPU usage dropped to idle, and
it never recovered. Root cause, strongly suspected (not fully traced
through every layer, but the numeric coincidence is compelling): 49.71
days is suspiciously almost exactly double the ~24.85-day ceiling of a
signed 32-bit millisecond timer interval, and this huge duration flows
straight into the renderer's own step-scheduling timer
(PlaybackEngineObject::scheduleNextStep() -> QChronoTimer) as well as
into Renderer::doNextStep()'s own m_sessionCtx.seekPos/lastFrameEnd
bookkeeping. FIX: added a second, independent cap right after computing
`start`/`end` in decodeSubtitle() -- clamp the effective duration to 60
seconds when it exceeds that, before the existing `end <= start`
rejection check ever runs. A bounded placeholder here is safe by
construction: the disc's own actual next event (new content, or a real
clear segment) always arrives with its own correct timestamp and
supersedes whatever is currently showing regardless of this value, so
the only possible downside is an early clear by a few seconds if the
true on-disc gap is longer than the cap -- never a stability or
correctness issue.

THIS FIX WAS REBUILT AND RE-TESTED, AND FOUND STILL BROKEN -- superseded
by PART 3 below, kept here for the historical record. The 60-second cap
did stop the outright infinite stall, but the user's own precise,
real-time observation of the rebuilt result (much more reliable than
this session's own coarse ~15-30s-interval screenshot sampling, which
initially and WRONGLY reported this as fully working) showed it was
still badly broken: the first subtitle event stayed on screen far too
long, most of chapter 1's real subtitle events were skipped/never shown
at all, the timing drifted increasingly late (by ~30 seconds within the
first few minutes), and -- critically -- video and audio both froze for
a few real seconds at EVERY subtitle transition.

PART 3 (real fix): root cause was that Part 2's long-duration content
frame, plus Qt's own synthetic "clear at end" frame right behind it in
the queue, PERMANENTLY BLOCKED every real, chronologically sooner
subtitle event that arrived later in decode order -- this Renderer's
frame queue is strict FIFO (Renderer::doNextStep() always operates on
frames.front()), so nothing behind that 60-second-out pair could render
until that slot was finally reached, and the pattern then repeated,
compounding the lag indefinitely. The periodic video/audio freezes at
each transition are very likely this same queue's bounded capacity
backing up and applying backpressure to the shared demuxer, blocking it
from advancing ANY stream (video/audio included) until this queue drains
a slot.

The actual fix has nothing to do with capping a duration at all: Qt's
subtitle sink state (QPlatformVideoSink::m_subtitleText/m_subtitleImage,
stamped onto every video frame by setVideoFrame()) is STICKY -- it
persists unchanged on screen until something calls
setSubtitleText()/setSubtitleImage() again, independent of any Frame's
own `duration`. So decodeSubtitle() now detects FFmpeg's own
end_display_time == UINT32_MAX sentinel PRECISELY (a new
`hasUnboundedDuration` bool, computed right where `end` itself is
computed, rather than inferring "too long" after the fact from an
arbitrary threshold the way Part 2 did) and, for that case only, gives
the frame a MINIMAL scheduling duration (`end = start +
TrackDuration(1)`, i.e. 1 microsecond -- just enough to satisfy the
existing `end <= start` rejection check while rendering essentially
instantly and never blocking the queue or polluting
Renderer::m_sessionCtx.seekPos/lastFrameEnd with a huge value) AND skips
emitting the redundant synthetic "clear at end" frame for this case
(emitting it would immediately re-clear the subtitle it just showed,
since `end` is now only 1us after `start`). The disc's own actual next
event -- new real content, or a genuine clear segment (num_rects == 0,
which also carries this same UINT32_MAX sentinel and produces an
empty-text/null-image Frame through this identical code path) -- arrives
later, in its own correct chronological position in the same queue, and
is what visually updates or clears the sticky sink state at the correct
disc-authored time. Nothing spans the gap artificially any more, so
nothing sits in the queue blocking later, sooner-arriving real events.

PARTIALLY VERIFIED 2026-08-17 (real rebuild, real disc, dense sampling
this time -- 20 screenshots at a fixed 6-second cadence over ~2 minutes
with the real vivace.exe process's own CPU time polled and logged at
every checkpoint, after the earlier sparse ~15-30s screenshot sampling
had produced a false "all clear" for Part 2): every one of the 20
position readouts advanced by ~6 real seconds with zero gaps and zero
repeated/stuck frames; CPU time climbed continuously at every checkpoint
(never flatlined, ruling out even a brief stall in any sampled window).
Three real dialogue/subtitle transitions fell within the sampled frames,
all APPEARING correct at this sampling density: a line appearing in sync
with a driving scene, correctly clearing on the next silent scenic shot,
and another line appearing in sync with a character's on-screen
introduction over a minute later -- no held-over stale text, no skipped
events, no gross (Part-2-scale) drift.

STILL NOT FULLY CORRECT after Part 3 -- the user's own real-time viewing
caught something a 6-second screenshot cadence could never catch: an
apparent GROWING subtitle-vs-audio delay, negligible at first,
accumulating to roughly a full second by about 1.5-2 minutes into using
the track. Before attempting a third blind fix, checked directly via
`ffprobe` against the real disc's own 00001.m2ts (a growing delay is the
classic signature of a wrong clock RATE): the PGS subtitle stream's
declared time_base is exactly 1/90000, IDENTICAL to video and audio --
ruling out a timebase-mismatch explanation for Part 1's pkt_timebase fix.
Added two TEMPORARY diagnostics (since removed, see PART 4 below):
decodeSubtitle() logging the computed `start` position per real content
event, and SubtitleRenderer::renderInternal() logging
Renderer::frameDelay(frame) -- an existing protected helper,
`now() - timeController.timeFromPosition(frame.absolutePts())` -- at the
moment each frame actually renders.

The resulting vivace.log evidence changed the diagnosis entirely: render
delay was consistently sub-2-milliseconds for EVERY event (0.13ms to
1.7ms, no growth at all) -- the renderer schedules essentially perfectly
against its own `start` value. The user then corrected their own earlier
report after watching more carefully: the real delay is a roughly
CONSTANT 0.5-1.5 SECONDS, present identically from the very FIRST
subtitle event (not growing) -- the "growing" appearance in the original
report was an artifact of imprecise perception, not a real trend.

PART 4, THE REAL FIX: a constant offset, with the renderer itself
confirmed near-perfectly on schedule, points squarely at the computed
`start`/`end` values themselves being offset from the true "position 0"
of the media -- and qffmpegtime_p.h has an EXPLICIT comment confirming
exactly this: "AVContextPosition ... position in the AVFormatContext, in
'1sec / AV_TIME_BASE' units ... The position is shifted on
AVFormatContext::start_time from TrackTime." FFmpeg's own
avcodec_decode_subtitle2() rescales `subtitle.pts` from
`avctx->pkt_timebase` to `AV_TIME_BASE_Q` (confirmed via real n7.1
source, libavcodec/decode.c) -- landing it in the AVFormatContext's raw
microsecond domain (AVContextPosition), NOT yet shifted to TrackPosition
convention. QFFmpeg::toTrackPosition() (used correctly elsewhere in this
same function, in the AV_NOPTS_VALUE branch, via
CodecContext::toTrackPosition()) applies exactly this shift for ordinary
video/audio stream positions -- but the `else` branch (subtitle.pts
valid, the one PGS/Blu-ray actually takes) computed `start`/`end`
directly from the raw rescaled value with NO such shift, a genuine,
pre-existing Qt Multimedia gap in this one branch. This only matters for
a container whose AVFormatContext::start_time is a real, nonzero value
large enough to notice -- routine for a BDAV .m2ts clip's own
PCR-derived base (not something broken about the disc), while most other
subtitle formats/containers this same branch also serves have
start_time == 0 and never exposed the gap.
FIX: `CodecContext` (qffmpegcodeccontext_p.h, a NEW file for this patch)
gained a `formatContext()` accessor (matching the existing `stream()`
accessor's style exactly). `decodeSubtitle()`'s `else` branch now
subtracts `toTrackDuration(contextStartOffset(m_codecContext.
formatContext()))` -- the exact same helper `toTrackPosition()` itself
uses internally -- from both `start` and `end`. Both temporary
diagnostics were removed once this root cause was confirmed via direct
source reading (qffmpegtime_p.h's own comment), not re-guessed.
Confirmed `qffmpegcodeccontext_p.h` isn't touched by any of the other
three qtmultimedia patches.
Part 4's Vivace-side companion fix (a new PlayerController::
m_deviceTrackSetupSource guard for the seek-reset regression, see Vivace's
own CLAUDE.md) VERIFIED end to end: selecting a subtitle track, then
seeking to a completely different scene, correctly leaves the track
selection (checkmark) intact instead of silently reverting to a default.

PART 5, a second real bug found by the user during that same seek test:
"You seeked slider when subtitle 'Written and Directed by / Hayao
Miyazaki' is shown. The text ... should be erased on seekbar's slider
movement, but it's kept showing (maybe) forever." Root cause: QVideoSink's
subtitle state (m_subtitleText/m_subtitleImage) is STICKY -- it only
changes when a subtitle Frame actually renders and calls
setSubtitleText()/setSubtitleImage() again. Renderer::seek() replaces
m_sessionCtx wholesale (discarding any frames still queued from before
the seek, confirmed by reading its real implementation), but nothing
told the SINK ITSELF that whatever it was last displaying is now stale --
so old text/image just sits there until some LATER real subtitle event
happens to arrive and overwrite it, which may not happen for a long time
(or ever, e.g. seeking into the final subtitle-free stretch of a film).
FIX: SubtitleRenderer (qffmpegsubtitlerenderer.cpp/_p.h, the header a NEW
file for this patch -- confirmed its one apparent match in
qtmultimedia-deinterlace.patch is a false positive, a filename mentioned
in an unrelated CMakeLists.txt source list, not a real diff hunk) now
overrides seekInternal() (Renderer's own empty-by-default seek hook,
called right after the frame queue is reset) to clear the sink -- mirrors
the exact same clear this class's own destructor already performs when
tearing down entirely. If a real subtitle event covers the new seek
position, decodeSubtitle()'s normal flow re-populates the sink correctly
once that frame is decoded and rendered, same as any ordinary seek.

Rebuilt and tested. PART 5 (seek-clear) CONFIRMED WORKING -- user's own
direct test: "Stale subtitle cleaning works well as expected." PART 4
(the constant-offset timing fix) was not reliably verified by this
session's own automated screenshot testing (real dialogue events have
wider/less sharply-bounded display windows than assumed, BD launch
overhead is inconsistent run to run, and the main seek bar is far too
coarse -- ~8 seconds per pixel on an 86-minute title -- for a 2-second
discriminating window), but the USER then confirmed it directly: "Part4
is also confirmed working well."

ALL FIVE PARTS OF THIS BLU-RAY PGS SUBTITLE FEATURE ARE NOW CONFIRMED
WORKING END TO END against real disc playback: Part 1 (pkt_timebase),
Part 3 (sticky-sink minimal-duration handling), Part 4 (the
AVFormatContext::start_time shift), and Part 5 (seekInternal() sink
clear) -- plus the independent Vivace-side m_deviceTrackSetupSource fix
for the seek-triggered track-selection reset regression (see Vivace's
own CLAUDE.md). Two intermediate fix attempts were tried and reverted/
superseded along the way (the original crash-fix's own guessed-duration
attempt, and Part 2's flat 60-second cap) before arriving at each real
root cause.
